# Commits and pull requests

An automated pipeline governs every PR. It reads your commit headers to decide which rules and engineering standards to review against — so the header is not cosmetic, it is routing.

## Commit format

```
<type>(<area>): <description>

type ::= feat | fix | refactor | perf | chore | docs | test | ci | build | revert
area ::= frontend | backend | mobile | ios | android | infrastructure | docs
```

Rules:

- Description is **imperative, lowercase, no trailing period** — `add dark-mode toggle`, not `Added dark-mode toggle.`
- The area must be the area you actually changed.
- One concern per commit.

```
✅ feat(backend): add payments endpoint
✅ fix(frontend): stop the toggle resetting on route change
❌ Added payments endpoint          → unroutable; the pipeline will reject the PR
❌ feat: add payments endpoint      → no area
❌ feature(backend): …              → "feature" is not a valid type
```

**A PR where neither any commit nor the PR title matches this grammar is rejected before review.** The pipeline cannot tell which standards apply, so it fails closed rather than guessing.

## Areas are detected from paths too

The pipeline unions the areas in your commit headers with the areas implied by the files you touched (`apps/*frontend*/**` → frontend, `apps/*backend*/**` → backend).

A PR labelled `feat(frontend)` that also edits `apps/api-backend/` **is reviewed against the backend standards as well**. You cannot narrow the review by how you word a commit — so label honestly, and if a change genuinely spans both areas, expect both checklists to apply.

## Pull requests

- One task, one PR. Link the task brief.
- Target the branch the pipeline governs (usually `main`). A PR aimed elsewhere is ignored entirely by the pipeline — no checks, no review.
- The description states **what changed, why, and how it was verified**. If part of the scope was deliberately left out, say so.
- Never push to the target branch directly.

## What the pipeline will do

Three checks appear on the PR, in order:

| Check | What it runs |
|---|---|
| `test` | The configured test gate for the PR's areas |
| `build` | The configured build gate (skipped if tests fail) |
| `ql-pipeline` | AI review against the rules **and** the stage checklists, then merge / fix / block |

Outcomes:

- **MERGE** — no blocking findings. Approved and merged automatically.
- **FIX** — blocking findings that are auto-fixable. A bot commit `fix(<area>): resolve pipeline complaint (attempt N) [bot]` is pushed and the PR is re-reviewed. Capped at 3 attempts.
- **BLOCK** — labelled `needs-human`. Read the request-changes review; it cites the exact rule or checklist section.

Findings cite their source: `backend.rules#no-string-concat-sql` (a rule) or `backend.standards#09-controllers` (a checklist section in `.standards/`). When a finding cites a standards section, open that section — the reviewer is quoting your own documentation back at you.

## Things that always require a human

PRs touching `.github/workflows/`, `.github/pipeline.config.yml`, or `.github/pipeline-rules/` are never auto-merged or auto-fixed. Expect `needs-human` and a manual review.
